WEBVTT

1
00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:38.920
What is that?

2
00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:41.799
It's that famous picture
of the monster.

3
00:01:42.135 --> 00:01:43.295
But it's fake.

4
00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.971
How do you know it's fake?
It looks real.

5
00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:48.605
Oh, it's fake all right.

6
00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:51.207
Of course it's fake,
everyone knows that.

7
00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:56.778
Wait now, son. There's more
to that photo than meets the eye.

8
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:00.550
- Oh, really?
- If you'd like to know the real truth...

9
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.447
Yeah. I wanna know.

10
00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.213
- Come on. It'll be fun.
- Come on.

11
00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:09.623
Well, you have to go back
to World War ll.

12
00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:13.363
The Germans had captured
the rest of Europe...

13
00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:18.560
...and were only a hundred miles or so
from these shores.

14
00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:22.033
And you need to know
about a young lad...

15
00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:25.769
...called Angus MacMorrow.

16
00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:30.173
The wee lad was drawn to water...

17
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:34.752
...but deathly afraid of it
at the same time.

18
00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:31.566
You know something, Angus?

19
00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:36.333
The tide pool
is a very strange, enchanted place.

20
00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:41.445
See these wee beauties?

21
00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:46.680
Now, they are full of luck and magic.

22
00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:48.615
- No!
- Aye, Angus.

23
00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:52.451
- You best believe it.
- Can I keep it, Daddy?

24
00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:56.291
Aye. But don't tell your Mummy.

25
00:04:56.496 --> 00:04:59.294
She believes things
should stay where they are.

26
00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:24.545
Angus.

27
00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:28.691
Angus.

28
00:05:32.031 --> 00:05:35.523
Angus MacMorrow,
what in heaven's name?

29
00:05:35.735 --> 00:05:39.068
- Sorry. I'm coming.
- Indeed you are.

30
00:05:44.243 --> 00:05:46.541
I thought you'd drowned.

31
00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:55.446
You didn't even get wet.

32
00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:09.428
That piece. Please.

33
00:06:10.536 --> 00:06:12.595
- Thank you.
- Cheerio.

34
00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:21.843
See you, Archie.

35
00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.649
I'd say it's going to rain,
Mrs. MacMorrow.

36
00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:27.445
Think you might be right,
Mr. McCowan.

37
00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:36.321
Would you remind me to tell Clyde
to move the cattle to the lower field?

38
00:06:57.617 --> 00:06:59.482
I'll just put my pail in the workshop.

39
00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:02.245
Well, be quick.
You have to help with the tea.

40
00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:08.757
- Clyde.
- Aye.

41
00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:11.452
Can we move the cattle
down to the lower field?

42
00:07:11.664 --> 00:07:14.758
Oh, aye.
I have got nothing better to do.

43
00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:17.868
Mummy. Look what I've got.

44
00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:23.197
- Where'd you get that?
- Every volunteer got four oranges.

45
00:07:23.409 --> 00:07:25.809
I haven't had an orange
in Lord knows how long.

46
00:07:26.012 --> 00:07:28.378
How was the shore?
Did Angus have fun?

47
00:07:28.581 --> 00:07:30.048
Angus, fun?

48
00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:41.571
Angus! Come on in now.

49
00:09:41.781 --> 00:09:44.249
You told me you'd help with the tea.

50
00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:01.131
All the Malay Peninsula
has been overrun.

51
00:10:01.333 --> 00:10:04.359
We are struggling hard
in the Libyan Desert...

52
00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:08.768
...where perhaps another
serious battle will be soon fought.

53
00:10:08.974 --> 00:10:11.534
The whole future of mankind...

54
00:10:11.744 --> 00:10:15.111
...may depend upon our actions
and upon our conduct.

55
00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:20.149
So far we have not failed.
We shall not fail now.

56
00:10:20.352 --> 00:10:24.288
Let us move forward
steadfastly together...

57
00:13:09.555 --> 00:13:11.318
Jings.

58
00:13:26.338 --> 00:13:29.535
It's all right. There, there.

59
00:14:02.708 --> 00:14:05.768
Here you go. Come on.

60
00:14:06.912 --> 00:14:08.504
That's it.

61
00:14:17.289 --> 00:14:20.224
Come on. Come on.

62
00:14:23.162 --> 00:14:25.096
That's it.

63
00:14:29.335 --> 00:14:30.893
All right, then.

64
00:14:32.571 --> 00:14:34.596
That's a good boy.

65
00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:40.203
It's all right.

66
00:14:51.924 --> 00:14:53.118
Oh, no.

67
00:15:12.644 --> 00:15:13.975
There you are.

68
00:15:19.651 --> 00:15:23.644
It's all right. I'll take care of you.

69
00:15:24.189 --> 00:15:25.178
Angus.

70
00:15:27.092 --> 00:15:30.425
Angus? Angus, it's bedtime.

71
00:15:30.629 --> 00:15:33.120
How many times
do I have to call you?

72
00:15:48.614 --> 00:15:50.445
Crikey.

73
00:16:03.062 --> 00:16:04.689
Get a move on!

74
00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:13.399
What is it?

75
00:16:14.573 --> 00:16:17.007
Maybe Daddy's with them.

76
00:16:24.316 --> 00:16:26.113
Pull up, lads.

77
00:16:31.723 --> 00:16:34.590
- Do you know anything about this?
- No.

78
00:16:35.327 --> 00:16:38.296
- They've got to be here for a reason.
- I don't know.

79
00:16:44.703 --> 00:16:46.432
Excuse me, sir.

80
00:16:48.207 --> 00:16:49.367
Good morning, madam.

81
00:16:49.575 --> 00:16:52.635
Captain Thomas Hamilton,
12 Medium Regiment, Royal Artillery.

82
00:16:52.845 --> 00:16:53.869
Are we all set?

83
00:16:55.514 --> 00:16:58.745
The housekeeper, Mrs. MacMorrow,
is making all the arrangements.

84
00:16:58.951 --> 00:17:01.010
- She is?
- Yes. Is she around?

85
00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:02.551
I'm Anne MacMorrow.

86
00:17:03.322 --> 00:17:04.846
Oh, I beg your pardon.

87
00:17:05.057 --> 00:17:09.289
Clearly, HQ have failed to notify you.
My troop is to be billeted here.

88
00:17:09.495 --> 00:17:11.258
What with?

89
00:17:11.463 --> 00:17:13.328
I've hardly any staff.

90
00:17:13.532 --> 00:17:17.024
When Lord Killin went off to the RAF,
only a few of us stayed on here.

91
00:17:17.236 --> 00:17:19.466
Well, we won't expect afternoon tea.

92
00:17:19.671 --> 00:17:22.572
No, only the officers
will be billeted inside the house.

93
00:17:22.774 --> 00:17:25.470
The rest of my men
will camp outside.

94
00:17:26.578 --> 00:17:29.570
Well, I'm afraid I really would need
Lord Killin's permission.

95
00:17:29.781 --> 00:17:30.770
Of course.

96
00:17:30.983 --> 00:17:32.974
- Wormsley?
- Yes, sir.

97
00:17:34.486 --> 00:17:39.549
From Lord Killin. We're friends,
actually. At Oxford the same year.

98
00:17:40.993 --> 00:17:42.483
All right.

99
00:17:42.694 --> 00:17:46.130
But I have to tell you, our cupboards
are not exactly full these days.

100
00:17:46.331 --> 00:17:49.391
Not to worry. The War Office
keeps us well-supplied.

101
00:17:49.601 --> 00:17:52.968
And what's more, we have a number
of keen hunters in our regiment.

102
00:17:53.172 --> 00:17:55.766
They are trained killers, after all.

103
00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:03.474
You see, Scotland is the front line
in the war these days.

104
00:18:04.116 --> 00:18:05.583
- Sir.
- Good man.

105
00:18:05.784 --> 00:18:08.252
Nazi submarines are everywhere.

106
00:18:08.453 --> 00:18:09.920
Here we are.

107
00:18:10.122 --> 00:18:13.148
Splendid. Yes.

108
00:18:15.327 --> 00:18:16.351
Well, how lovely.

109
00:18:17.696 --> 00:18:20.790
Now all this, you see,
is for your own protection.

110
00:18:20.999 --> 00:18:22.933
- Goldman?
- Well, we're very grateful.

111
00:18:23.135 --> 00:18:25.365
Is this the German advance?

112
00:18:25.571 --> 00:18:28.131
Following the troop movements,
are you?

113
00:18:30.676 --> 00:18:34.339
Don't you worry, son.
We're ready for them.

114
00:18:36.448 --> 00:18:38.916
- Now, you men, come with me.
- Sir.

115
00:18:39.117 --> 00:18:41.449
Can you put my desk over here?

116
00:18:41.753 --> 00:18:44.483
Kirstie, Angus, run along.

117
00:18:44.690 --> 00:18:48.285
We don't want to distract our guests
from their duties.

118
00:18:48.961 --> 00:18:50.326
Can you keep a secret?

119
00:18:50.529 --> 00:18:53.965
Isn't this exciting?
Soldiers right here in our home.

120
00:18:54.166 --> 00:18:57.397
- Promise me you can keep a secret.
- What are you babbling about?

121
00:18:57.603 --> 00:19:01.903
- You have to swear not to tell Mummy.
- I'll not breathe a word. What is it?

122
00:19:03.275 --> 00:19:04.264
I don't know.

123
00:19:36.608 --> 00:19:40.510
Hey! Those are my daddy's boots.
Let go.

124
00:19:45.984 --> 00:19:48.509
Bad boy. You can't eat them.

125
00:19:51.857 --> 00:19:53.848
But I suppose you are hungry.

126
00:20:02.467 --> 00:20:04.560
What's that? Churchill.

127
00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:06.294
That's enough, Churchill.

128
00:20:06.505 --> 00:20:08.598
- Who are you?
- Sergeant Wallace Strunk.

129
00:20:08.807 --> 00:20:12.243
Twelve Medium Regiment, Royal
Artillery. First battery cook.

130
00:20:12.444 --> 00:20:14.071
Churchill, regiment mascot.

131
00:20:17.015 --> 00:20:20.007
- Unauthorized personnel.
- I just wanted a wee snack.

132
00:20:20.218 --> 00:20:22.846
Provisions dispensed
only by written orders in triplicate.

133
00:20:23.055 --> 00:20:24.852
Do you have the paperwork?

134
00:20:25.624 --> 00:20:27.182
Then off you go.

135
00:20:27.392 --> 00:20:31.829
Who's Daddy's boy, then, eh?
Good boy. Good Churchill.

136
00:20:32.331 --> 00:20:34.492
- You're still here?
- I take out the rubbish.

137
00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:35.962
On the double, then.

138
00:20:37.569 --> 00:20:41.528
Who's the best little soldier in the
regiment, then? It's my Churchill.

139
00:20:41.740 --> 00:20:45.073
Good boy. Give me a kiss.
Give me another kiss.

140
00:20:55.954 --> 00:20:57.285
Hello?

141
00:20:57.489 --> 00:20:59.480
Where are you, boy?

142
00:21:03.895 --> 00:21:05.954
Where are you hiding?

143
00:21:12.938 --> 00:21:16.169
You are a sneaky devil,
aren't you, boy?

144
00:21:16.475 --> 00:21:17.840
If you are a boy.

145
00:21:18.377 --> 00:21:20.368
What can we call you?

146
00:21:31.556 --> 00:21:34.650
Crusoe. Trapped on an island.

147
00:21:35.594 --> 00:21:38.757
Right, then, Crusoe.
Let's see what you are.

148
00:21:52.377 --> 00:21:53.867
No.

149
00:22:00.085 --> 00:22:01.575
No.

150
00:22:05.223 --> 00:22:06.747
There you are.

151
00:22:11.463 --> 00:22:13.021
Maybe you're a mammal.

152
00:22:21.573 --> 00:22:23.632
Where were you born, Daddy?

153
00:22:23.842 --> 00:22:27.437
I was born and brought up
on the banks of Loch Morar.

154
00:22:27.913 --> 00:22:32.680
People whispered about a beastie
living in that very loch.

155
00:22:33.018 --> 00:22:37.421
Now, always there've been tales
of sightings of such a creature...

156
00:22:37.622 --> 00:22:41.991
...sometimes at sea,
but more often in a loch.

157
00:22:42.994 --> 00:22:44.894
When I was a boy...

158
00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:48.126
...how I longed to see that monster.

159
00:22:49.501 --> 00:22:51.969
When I was a boy...

160
00:22:53.271 --> 00:22:55.569
...terrible times they were.

161
00:22:55.774 --> 00:22:58.766
Boys and girls, wives and mothers
all over the world...

162
00:22:58.977 --> 00:23:00.945
...left without the man of the house.

163
00:23:02.013 --> 00:23:07.849
They all went off to war,
and many never came back.

164
00:23:08.787 --> 00:23:11.984
We're a tiny village
in the wilds of Scotland...

165
00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:14.886
...but 20 men we lost.

166
00:23:15.293 --> 00:23:17.386
But maybe I'm boring you
with my story.

167
00:23:18.296 --> 00:23:20.594
No. No, please. Don't stop.

168
00:23:21.066 --> 00:23:23.398
Okay, if you insist.

169
00:23:24.202 --> 00:23:27.194
Angus was just like
every other wee boy.

170
00:23:27.405 --> 00:23:30.238
He wanted his daddy home again.

171
00:23:31.543 --> 00:23:35.604
But he also had another problem.

172
00:23:37.516 --> 00:23:41.145
Crusoe, you ate it all.
Come out. You'll be sick.

173
00:23:50.795 --> 00:23:54.925
That's a good boy.
I'm sorry for grabbing.

174
00:23:56.101 --> 00:23:57.966
You're all dried up.

175
00:24:14.519 --> 00:24:16.009
You'll like this.

176
00:24:20.725 --> 00:24:21.987
That's better, eh?

177
00:24:26.565 --> 00:24:28.726
- Look at that.
- Oh, yeah.

178
00:24:28.934 --> 00:24:31.994
Go in there.
Oh, another five-pounder.

179
00:24:32.203 --> 00:24:33.761
Oh, you beauty.

180
00:24:33.972 --> 00:24:36.736
- Aye.
- That takes care of my lunch.

181
00:24:51.156 --> 00:24:52.885
What are they doing
up on that hill?

182
00:24:53.959 --> 00:24:56.553
It's a secret mission.
They're after submarines.

183
00:24:56.761 --> 00:24:59.559
Well, if it's secret,
how do you know about it?

184
00:24:59.764 --> 00:25:02.130
- I have my ways, my contacts.
- Aye.

185
00:25:02.334 --> 00:25:06.327
Well, make contact with them oars.
Pull us over some more fish.

186
00:25:18.216 --> 00:25:20.810
Right. Salute.

187
00:25:22.354 --> 00:25:23.821
Down.

188
00:25:24.022 --> 00:25:25.785
Map, sir?

189
00:25:32.163 --> 00:25:35.758
- Perfect.
- It's an amazing view, sir.

190
00:25:36.835 --> 00:25:41.636
HMS Royal Oak was lost
to a German U-boat in Scapa Flow...

191
00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:43.865
...not a hundred miles from this spot.

192
00:25:44.609 --> 00:25:47.077
Eight hundred and thirty-three
sailors lost.

193
00:25:48.947 --> 00:25:51.006
We're not here for the scenery,
Wormsley.

194
00:25:51.216 --> 00:25:52.740
Yes, sir.

195
00:25:54.686 --> 00:25:56.813
Now, make no mistake...

196
00:25:57.922 --> 00:25:59.913
...we are on the front line here.

197
00:26:00.625 --> 00:26:04.254
Now, I have studied
the German advance.

198
00:26:04.529 --> 00:26:08.431
If they invade,
they will come right through here.

199
00:26:09.034 --> 00:26:12.299
This is the deepest loch in Scotland,
perfect for their troopships.

200
00:26:12.504 --> 00:26:15.371
But they will send submarines first
for reconnaissance.

201
00:26:15.573 --> 00:26:17.666
Won't the steel net stop them, sir?

202
00:26:18.843 --> 00:26:20.333
The submarine net will be down...

203
00:26:21.012 --> 00:26:24.243
...to permit passage
by routine marine traffic...

204
00:26:24.449 --> 00:26:26.781
...and to let their subs in.

205
00:26:28.086 --> 00:26:30.554
And then we will raise the net...

206
00:26:31.923 --> 00:26:34.050
...and we will destroy them.

207
00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:46.500
I wish I could swim like you.
I wish I wasn't so afraid.

208
00:26:46.705 --> 00:26:48.969
Come on! Stop!

209
00:26:50.575 --> 00:26:52.770
What the hell
you think you're doing?

210
00:26:54.512 --> 00:26:56.275
- What's going on?
- Keep your hands off!

211
00:26:56.481 --> 00:26:57.880
You're not fit to touch it.

212
00:26:58.083 --> 00:26:59.573
Oh, no.

213
00:26:59.784 --> 00:27:02.719
I better go. Stay out of trouble.

214
00:27:08.493 --> 00:27:10.222
Look where you're going.

215
00:27:10.428 --> 00:27:12.760
Where'd you learn to drive,
in the Dodgems?

216
00:27:12.964 --> 00:27:16.024
Look, there's a rear-vision mirror.
Know what that's for?

217
00:27:16.234 --> 00:27:19.635
- That's to look where you're going.
- It was an accident, I assure you.

218
00:27:19.838 --> 00:27:21.965
All right, Clyde. That's enough.

219
00:27:22.173 --> 00:27:23.834
That's a lovely bit of meat there.

220
00:27:25.276 --> 00:27:27.039
Who's this?

221
00:27:33.351 --> 00:27:35.046
There he goes.

222
00:27:46.631 --> 00:27:49.657
Did you see all those jeeps and
lorries going up and down the hill?

223
00:27:49.868 --> 00:27:50.857
You're late.

224
00:27:51.069 --> 00:27:52.900
And that is very hush-hush.

225
00:27:53.104 --> 00:27:56.870
Aye. It's a secret mission,
that's what I hear. Top secret.

226
00:27:57.075 --> 00:27:58.235
I'll say one thing, Jimmy.

227
00:27:58.443 --> 00:28:01.776
If it's a secret, you'd be
the last person to tell about it.

228
00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:04.574
- That's cruel. That's very cruel.
- Aye.

229
00:28:10.388 --> 00:28:11.514
- Evening.
- All right.

230
00:28:11.723 --> 00:28:14.123
- What'll you have?
- Pint of McEwan's.

231
00:28:23.635 --> 00:28:24.795
Looks like rain.

232
00:28:26.671 --> 00:28:28.298
Yeah, it might.

233
00:28:28.506 --> 00:28:31.475
You're not from the village.
Would you be with the artillery?

234
00:28:32.377 --> 00:28:35.813
Here we are, lads.
Our home away from home.

235
00:28:36.314 --> 00:28:40.011
Did you hear the captain today?
"We're on the front lines. "

236
00:28:40.218 --> 00:28:41.412
Front line, my aunt.

237
00:28:41.619 --> 00:28:44.520
We're here because his daddy
put him as far away from danger...

238
00:28:44.722 --> 00:28:46.622
...as the War Office could find.

239
00:28:46.825 --> 00:28:48.952
You're the boys
with the big gun up the loch.

240
00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:52.618
- Indeed we are.
- Would that thing sink a submarine?

241
00:28:52.831 --> 00:28:54.765
With one shell.

242
00:28:55.400 --> 00:28:57.595
- What are you having, lads?
- Pint of heavy.

243
00:28:57.802 --> 00:29:00.202
Yeah, I'll have a whiskey.
What you got there?

244
00:29:02.841 --> 00:29:05.639
Recock, fire.

245
00:29:05.844 --> 00:29:08.745
Recock, fire.

246
00:29:08.947 --> 00:29:11.780
Recock, fire.

247
00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:38.166
Angus, we need to talk.

248
00:30:42.874 --> 00:30:44.239
Mrs. MacMorrow?

249
00:30:45.043 --> 00:30:48.672
- Who are you?
- Me name's Lewis Mowbray.

250
00:30:49.580 --> 00:30:51.707
Oh, yes. The handyman.

251
00:30:53.618 --> 00:30:55.745
You're two days late in arriving.

252
00:30:57.021 --> 00:30:58.750
Yes, ma'am.

253
00:30:58.957 --> 00:31:00.322
I...

254
00:31:01.392 --> 00:31:04.759
I was... I...

255
00:31:06.264 --> 00:31:08.357
Well, you can start
by cleaning this mess.

256
00:31:10.068 --> 00:31:13.663
You can pack up
all these books and shells...

257
00:31:13.871 --> 00:31:16.635
...and charts and so forth.

258
00:31:17.075 --> 00:31:21.034
I want to empty this shop out.
And...

259
00:31:21.245 --> 00:31:23.873
...I think my son has an animal
hidden in here somewhere.

260
00:31:24.082 --> 00:31:25.982
He's not allowed pets, so...

261
00:31:26.184 --> 00:31:29.745
- Do you think you could manage that?
- Aye.

262
00:31:32.523 --> 00:31:34.821
And we'll have no drinking
in our house, Mr. Lewis.

263
00:31:35.526 --> 00:31:38.654
Yes, ma'am. That's-

264
00:31:38.863 --> 00:31:41.161
That's Mowbray, ma'am.

265
00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:43.658
Yes.

266
00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:03.983
Oh, no.

267
00:32:04.422 --> 00:32:07.949
No! No, no! No!

268
00:32:08.159 --> 00:32:11.617
What are you doing?
Why did you empty the barrel?

269
00:32:11.829 --> 00:32:16.823
- Did you see anything fall out of it?
- Only water. What are you looking for?

270
00:32:17.368 --> 00:32:19.928
Nothing.
You'd remember if you'd seen it.

271
00:32:20.138 --> 00:32:22.129
I didn't see anything.

272
00:32:22.640 --> 00:32:24.198
Who are you?

273
00:32:24.409 --> 00:32:26.934
Mrs. MacMorrow hired me
to help her in the lodge.

274
00:32:27.145 --> 00:32:28.874
So this'll be my workshop now.

275
00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:31.480
I don't want you coming in
and out of it. Understand?

276
00:32:31.682 --> 00:32:35.140
This is my father's workshop.
He's off fighting in the war.

277
00:32:35.353 --> 00:32:36.980
- You can't just come in here-
- Lad!

278
00:32:37.321 --> 00:32:39.983
When your father returns,
he can do with it what he wants.

279
00:32:40.191 --> 00:32:42.284
But for now, this shop is mine...

280
00:32:42.493 --> 00:32:44.654
...and I want to be left alone.
Am I clear?

281
00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:47.661
Have I made myself clear?

282
00:33:00.778 --> 00:33:02.439
Crusoe?

283
00:33:02.647 --> 00:33:04.842
Crusoe, come out.

284
00:33:05.049 --> 00:33:07.210
Where are you hiding?

285
00:33:07.819 --> 00:33:09.980
Could I trouble you for a light?

286
00:33:14.358 --> 00:33:15.825
Thank you.

287
00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:21.325
Absolutely stunning countryside,
isn't it?

288
00:33:24.969 --> 00:33:26.664
You from around here?

289
00:33:27.305 --> 00:33:29.034
I'm from Dornoch.

290
00:33:29.841 --> 00:33:31.570
Captain Thomas Hamilton.

291
00:33:32.443 --> 00:33:34.035
Good to meet you.

292
00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:36.538
And yours?

293
00:33:39.684 --> 00:33:42.050
Mowbray. Lewis Mowbray.

294
00:33:45.389 --> 00:33:48.517
And you've seen service, I presume.

295
00:33:49.227 --> 00:33:51.889
Crusoe, please come out.

296
00:33:52.096 --> 00:33:54.030
Come out now.

297
00:34:02.473 --> 00:34:05.499
Well, have a good evening,
Mowbray.

298
00:34:07.745 --> 00:34:10.407
Where are you?
Where have you gone?

299
00:34:11.983 --> 00:34:14.781
Crusoe, where the devil are you?

300
00:35:21.919 --> 00:35:24.114
Sergeant Strunk!

301
00:35:28.192 --> 00:35:30.057
Bad dog, Churchill.

302
00:35:30.261 --> 00:35:33.594
Don't care if you're Lord Chamberlain,
I won't have my kitchen destroyed.

303
00:35:33.798 --> 00:35:37.325
Sorry, ma'am,
I don't know what got into him.

304
00:36:04.695 --> 00:36:07.562
Hush your wheesht, you daft dog!

305
00:36:20.911 --> 00:36:23.038
Crusoe, no!

306
00:36:25.116 --> 00:36:27.584
- Stop. Get back in there.
- No. No, it's horrible.

307
00:36:27.785 --> 00:36:30.549
- He won't hurt you. Believe me.
- It's disgusting.

308
00:36:30.755 --> 00:36:32.620
- He won't hurt you.
- What is it?

309
00:36:32.990 --> 00:36:37.017
I don't know, but it came from an egg.
I tried to tell you.

310
00:36:41.599 --> 00:36:43.794
Jings, Crusoe.

311
00:36:44.001 --> 00:36:45.593
You're as big as a prize pig.

312
00:36:46.904 --> 00:36:49.395
How'd you grow so fast?

313
00:36:50.274 --> 00:36:52.401
I thought I'd lost you.

314
00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:55.806
He's very friendly. Aren't you, boy?

315
00:36:56.013 --> 00:36:57.947
Angus, Mummy will have a fit.

316
00:36:58.149 --> 00:37:01.277
Don't you dare tell her.
Kirstie, please.

317
00:37:01.485 --> 00:37:05.046
He needs me. I'm his only friend.

318
00:37:06.557 --> 00:37:08.582
I saved his life.

319
00:37:17.201 --> 00:37:19.135
The poor wee thing.

320
00:37:21.339 --> 00:37:22.829
You won't tell?

321
00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:26.305
- Swear.
- I don't swear, Angus MacMorrow.

322
00:37:26.510 --> 00:37:28.603
I'll not speak a word.

323
00:37:29.146 --> 00:37:31.740
You're a good sister after all.

324
00:37:34.952 --> 00:37:35.036
Every 10 minutes...

325
00:37:35.136 --> 00:37:36.917
Every 10 minutes...

326
00:37:37.988 --> 00:37:39.683
Good morning, ma'am.

327
00:37:42.426 --> 00:37:45.589
- Lovely morning, miss.
- Morning.

328
00:37:51.635 --> 00:37:53.967
- Oh, God. I'm sorry.
- It's all right. Come in.

329
00:37:56.874 --> 00:37:58.341
Come in.

330
00:38:07.051 --> 00:38:10.179
You've done some very good work
around the grounds, Mr. Lewis.

331
00:38:11.489 --> 00:38:15.016
Thank you, ma'am.
And it's Mowbray, ma'am.

332
00:38:16.293 --> 00:38:18.022
Oh, yes.

333
00:38:18.462 --> 00:38:22.990
Did you know the guest bathroom's
been out of order for three days now?

334
00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:24.997
I'll see to it immediately, ma'am.

335
00:38:26.937 --> 00:38:30.168
I have some clothes. My husband's.

336
00:38:30.374 --> 00:38:33.502
Well, you seem to be about the
same size. Would you care for them?

337
00:38:35.780 --> 00:38:38.340
Well, won't he need them
when he comes home?

338
00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:43.047
He's not coming home,
Mr. Mowbray.

339
00:38:44.021 --> 00:38:47.957
His ship was sunk at sea.
It's been almost a year.

340
00:38:51.295 --> 00:38:54.856
I'm sorry. Sorry.

341
00:38:55.232 --> 00:38:56.597
It's just-

342
00:38:56.801 --> 00:39:00.897
It's just that your son had said that...

343
00:39:02.940 --> 00:39:05.170
Angus is a confused boy.

344
00:39:05.743 --> 00:39:06.869
Confused?

345
00:39:09.213 --> 00:39:10.908
Yes.

346
00:39:12.850 --> 00:39:16.286
I have a lot to do.
Do you want them or not?

347
00:39:16.987 --> 00:39:20.047
Yes. Thank you.

348
00:39:22.393 --> 00:39:24.156
Aye.

349
00:39:25.463 --> 00:39:27.158
Thanks.

350
00:39:54.091 --> 00:39:56.025
Did you have to
turn him into a mummy?

351
00:39:56.227 --> 00:39:58.889
It was getting too loud.
Somebody would've heard.

352
00:40:01.632 --> 00:40:03.725
Quiet, Crusoe!

353
00:40:04.835 --> 00:40:05.824
Who's there?

354
00:40:06.804 --> 00:40:08.396
I'm here to fix the bathroom.

355
00:40:08.806 --> 00:40:11.604
- We're using it.
- What, both of you?

356
00:40:11.809 --> 00:40:13.777
But I thought it was out of order.

357
00:40:14.211 --> 00:40:15.610
What's that noise?

358
00:40:18.048 --> 00:40:20.016
I have a cough.

359
00:40:20.818 --> 00:40:22.843
You got an animal in there?

360
00:40:23.053 --> 00:40:26.511
Look, you better let me in
or your mother will hear about this.

361
00:40:26.891 --> 00:40:29.086
- No.
- Let me in.

362
00:40:39.470 --> 00:40:42.200
Listen, you two, all right?
I have to do my job.

363
00:40:42.606 --> 00:40:45.336
Your mother told me this bathroom
has been out of order-

364
00:40:45.543 --> 00:40:46.805
What- What is that?

365
00:40:48.245 --> 00:40:52.375
He came from an egg at the tide pool.
His name's Crusoe.

366
00:40:58.355 --> 00:41:00.550
What in the name of-?

367
00:41:02.626 --> 00:41:04.685
He's very friendly.

368
00:41:18.609 --> 00:41:20.804
Mary and Joseph.

369
00:41:21.011 --> 00:41:24.378
- He almost died when he was born.
- Almost took my hand off.

370
00:41:24.815 --> 00:41:26.646
He thinks I'm his father.

371
00:41:30.487 --> 00:41:32.455
I wonder...

372
00:41:32.656 --> 00:41:35.318
- It just can't be.
- Can't be what?

373
00:41:35.793 --> 00:41:38.125
Well, it looks like a...

374
00:41:39.029 --> 00:41:42.226
- Like a bloody water horse.
- A what?

375
00:41:43.167 --> 00:41:47.069
They're make-believe.
A legend from the Celtic past.

376
00:41:47.671 --> 00:41:50.196
They're said to be
the rarest of all creatures.

377
00:41:50.407 --> 00:41:54.707
There can be only one water horse
in the world at a time.

378
00:41:55.145 --> 00:41:59.548
When the one grows old,
it lays a single egg, and then it dies.

379
00:42:00.017 --> 00:42:02.417
- Crusoe's a girl?
- No.

380
00:42:02.620 --> 00:42:04.918
No, lad, he's girl and boy both.

381
00:42:05.122 --> 00:42:07.955
The beast is both mum and dad
to the egg.

382
00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:13.662
The old water horse dies
before the egg hatches...

383
00:42:13.864 --> 00:42:17.027
...so the new water horse
is born an orphan.

384
00:42:18.068 --> 00:42:19.968
Oh, that's sad.

385
00:42:23.140 --> 00:42:25.540
It's an amazing thing though, isn't it?

386
00:42:25.743 --> 00:42:27.938
Angus! Kirstie!

387
00:42:28.145 --> 00:42:30.204
Don't tell her.
She'd make us get rid of it.

388
00:42:30.414 --> 00:42:31.904
Angus?

389
00:42:32.516 --> 00:42:33.915
Will you help us, Mr. Lewis?

390
00:42:36.387 --> 00:42:38.617
You're asking me
to deceive your mother.

391
00:42:39.590 --> 00:42:41.990
Angus? Kirstie?

392
00:42:46.430 --> 00:42:49.888
- What are you doing in there?
- I was helping Mr. Lewis fix the pipes.

393
00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:52.466
Since when have pipes
been an interest of yours?

394
00:42:53.003 --> 00:42:56.734
- Wait! It's a bad time to interrupt him.
- What?

395
00:42:56.940 --> 00:43:00.000
Plumbing is tricky. It's like surgery.

396
00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:02.269
One mistake could mean death.

397
00:43:04.782 --> 00:43:06.647
Mr. Lewis found the problem.

398
00:43:12.356 --> 00:43:14.347
I can explain. He-

399
00:43:18.729 --> 00:43:20.993
Always been handy
with the plumbing.

400
00:43:21.365 --> 00:43:23.162
He's a genius.

401
00:43:23.367 --> 00:43:25.164
And you helped, did you, Angus?

402
00:43:25.369 --> 00:43:26.358
Yes, I-

403
00:43:26.570 --> 00:43:29.334
- I handed the tools.
- And you, Kirstie?

404
00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:32.998
No. I came to get Angus to show him
the civil assistance manual.

405
00:43:36.980 --> 00:43:40.677
It's getting a bit cramped in here,
so off you go. Did a grand job, Angus.

406
00:43:41.351 --> 00:43:43.114
Bye.

407
00:43:46.924 --> 00:43:48.755
It's the pipes.

408
00:43:48.959 --> 00:43:52.861
It's the air locks bubbling through,
you see.

409
00:43:54.031 --> 00:43:55.726
Angus was a great help.

410
00:43:57.034 --> 00:43:58.899
So you said.

411
00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:01.501
Oh, dear. Excuse me. That's-

412
00:44:01.705 --> 00:44:04.640
The doctor warned me
not to eat haggis for breakfast.

413
00:44:04.842 --> 00:44:06.400
So it's something about the...

414
00:44:06.777 --> 00:44:11.009
...texture of the innards mixed in
with the suet that I just can't...

415
00:44:11.215 --> 00:44:14.707
Thank you, thank you. You've
explained that quite enough.

416
00:44:18.088 --> 00:44:23.788
Shut your ugly gob. You bloody near
got me sacked, you wee ingrate.

417
00:44:31.835 --> 00:44:34.702
Captain, we'll have that bathroom
up and running by tonight.

418
00:44:34.905 --> 00:44:36.634
Oh, wonderful.

419
00:44:36.940 --> 00:44:39.568
Would you allow me to organize
a meal tomorrow evening?

420
00:44:39.777 --> 00:44:41.074
As a token of our gratitude.

421
00:44:41.278 --> 00:44:44.179
Just the officers and yourself,
of course, as guest of honor.

422
00:44:48.018 --> 00:44:50.179
Thank you very much.
That'd be very nice.

423
00:44:50.554 --> 00:44:53.284
Excellent. Shall we say 8:00?

424
00:44:56.994 --> 00:44:58.393
What are we going to do?

425
00:44:58.595 --> 00:45:01.257
One thing's sure, you need to
get him out of the house.

426
00:45:01.465 --> 00:45:03.763
- But where can I put him?
- In the loch.

427
00:45:03.967 --> 00:45:06.731
No! I want Crusoe to stay.
He's mine!

428
00:45:06.937 --> 00:45:10.031
But he can't spend his life
in a toilet bowl.

429
00:45:10.641 --> 00:45:13.075
- But he can stay with me.
- Whatever the devil he is...

430
00:45:13.277 --> 00:45:14.972
...he's meant to swim and be free.

431
00:45:16.079 --> 00:45:17.876
The loch is loaded
with salmon to eat...

432
00:45:18.081 --> 00:45:21.312
...and there's plenty of water for him
to grow and move around in.

433
00:45:21.518 --> 00:45:25.284
- You want what's best for him?
- What's best for him is to be with me.

434
00:45:51.215 --> 00:45:55.015
Excuse me, sir. Mrs. MacMorrow.

435
00:45:55.652 --> 00:45:57.745
Anyone for champagne?

436
00:46:00.624 --> 00:46:02.922
- Thank you.
- That was very good, sir.

437
00:46:04.027 --> 00:46:07.053
It was brilliant tonight,
with the last change from the-

438
00:46:10.534 --> 00:46:12.627
Mrs. MacMorrow, you look radiant.

439
00:46:13.737 --> 00:46:16.934
In Scotland
we call that a fib, captain.

440
00:46:17.774 --> 00:46:19.674
Let me introduce you.

441
00:46:28.919 --> 00:46:30.944
I love to watch a man work.

442
00:46:36.426 --> 00:46:40.055
- Go easy.
- Don't we deserve it, eh?

443
00:46:52.910 --> 00:46:54.241
Gentlemen.

444
00:46:58.382 --> 00:47:01.909
The king, the regiment...

445
00:47:02.753 --> 00:47:04.448
...and the lady of the house.

446
00:47:04.655 --> 00:47:09.456
The king, the regiment
and the lady of the house.

447
00:47:09.826 --> 00:47:14.889
Well, gentlemen, technically speaking
I'm not actually the lady of the house...

448
00:47:15.098 --> 00:47:18.659
...but I suppose for tonight
I'm happy enough to serve that role.

449
00:47:19.102 --> 00:47:20.899
- Thank you.
- She's charming.

450
00:47:21.405 --> 00:47:24.203
Here you are. Look what I've got.

451
00:47:26.843 --> 00:47:29.004
Don't be greedy now.

452
00:47:36.086 --> 00:47:39.317
Angus, come quickly.
I've something funny to show you.

453
00:47:39.523 --> 00:47:42.083
I'll be back in a minute, Crusoe.

454
00:47:44.795 --> 00:47:46.558
You're not going to believe this.

455
00:47:56.707 --> 00:47:59.198
You're a fine woman, Gracie.

456
00:48:52.863 --> 00:48:54.626
Gracie!

457
00:48:56.133 --> 00:48:58.397
Oh, my God.
I don't know what came over me.

458
00:48:58.602 --> 00:48:59.694
Just a bit of fun.

459
00:49:03.407 --> 00:49:04.635
Where's the dog?

460
00:49:47.317 --> 00:49:48.375
Oh, no.

461
00:50:04.568 --> 00:50:07.901
Do you know why I take two pairs
of trousers onto the golf course?

462
00:50:08.105 --> 00:50:10.164
In case I get a hole in one.

463
00:50:43.773 --> 00:50:45.764
Did you hear something?

464
00:50:46.409 --> 00:50:50.072
No, no. Maybe this old house
has one or two ghosts, huh?

465
00:50:50.280 --> 00:50:53.738
Oh, I don't believe in such things,
captain.

466
00:50:56.553 --> 00:50:58.953
Come here, you bloody mongrel.

467
00:51:00.090 --> 00:51:01.580
I'm sure I can hear something.

468
00:51:01.791 --> 00:51:03.782
Yes, that time
I distinctly heard something.

469
00:51:15.872 --> 00:51:17.499
Crusoe, no!

470
00:51:21.278 --> 00:51:22.870
What's that now?

471
00:51:33.323 --> 00:51:35.689
Come on, Crusoe! Get out of here!

472
00:51:38.862 --> 00:51:41.524
- Take your hand off me, man.
- Angus, come right back here!

473
00:51:47.671 --> 00:51:49.832
Crusoe, come back!

474
00:51:58.715 --> 00:52:01.809
Mr. Lewis, what in goodness sake?

475
00:52:02.018 --> 00:52:04.714
Sorry, the name's Mowbray, ma'am.

476
00:52:04.988 --> 00:52:06.751
- What?
- Sorry, ma'am.

477
00:52:06.957 --> 00:52:09.357
The dog got loose,
and I was trying to catch him.

478
00:52:09.559 --> 00:52:11.459
I should say you failed at that.

479
00:52:13.597 --> 00:52:16.691
- Yes, ma'am.
- You think it's funny, Mowbray?

480
00:52:18.068 --> 00:52:20.662
No, no. It's a very serious matter.

481
00:52:20.870 --> 00:52:22.838
You hold your tongue, man.

482
00:52:23.039 --> 00:52:26.839
You're not in your stately home now.
I'll not be taking orders from you.

483
00:52:27.377 --> 00:52:29.038
That is quite enough.

484
00:52:29.246 --> 00:52:32.010
Go and collect Angus and
take him to his room immediately.

485
00:52:32.215 --> 00:52:33.876
Yes, ma'am.

486
00:53:07.350 --> 00:53:11.912
Crusoe? Crusoe, come back.
We're all going to get into trouble.

487
00:53:12.122 --> 00:53:14.818
Lewis, we've got to find Crusoe.
He's gone.

488
00:53:15.025 --> 00:53:18.859
Yeah, I know. I'll find the wee devil,
and you go straight to your room.

489
00:53:19.062 --> 00:53:20.620
- But, Lewis-
- Off to bed with you.

490
00:53:20.830 --> 00:53:24.231
Or your mom will have both our hides.
On you go. Go.

491
00:53:34.978 --> 00:53:39.347
Where are you,
you wee, smelly beast?

492
00:54:02.839 --> 00:54:05.603
Angus. Angus.
Angus, wake up. I found him.

493
00:54:05.809 --> 00:54:07.777
Can't we just
put him back in the bathtub?

494
00:54:07.977 --> 00:54:09.842
No. No, I don't think so.

495
00:54:12.849 --> 00:54:14.282
Jings.

496
00:54:17.854 --> 00:54:21.119
Lewis, do you know anything more
about water horses?

497
00:54:21.324 --> 00:54:25.090
I think it was- Aye, it was my Uncle
Lachlan who first told me about them.

498
00:54:25.295 --> 00:54:27.695
Many years ago,
there was a lost traveler...

499
00:54:27.897 --> 00:54:32.391
...and he's weeping bitter tears
because he cannot find his way home.

500
00:54:32.602 --> 00:54:33.899
He'd given up hope.

501
00:54:34.104 --> 00:54:39.132
Then suddenly, who should appear at
the bank of the loch to this traveler...

502
00:54:39.342 --> 00:54:43.802
...but a sea spirit. A water horse.

503
00:54:44.013 --> 00:54:48.780
The traveler, no fool is he, knows the
water horse is a creature of magic...

504
00:54:48.985 --> 00:54:52.785
...one that could grow
10 feet tall overnight.

505
00:54:53.656 --> 00:54:59.253
So the traveler, he feeds
the water horse, strokes it...

506
00:54:59.462 --> 00:55:03.660
...flatters it with compliments.
And finally the traveler says:

507
00:55:03.867 --> 00:55:07.894
"Incidentally, water horse,
my dear friend...

508
00:55:08.104 --> 00:55:11.039
...I'm wondering, if you've
no other pressing engagements...

509
00:55:11.241 --> 00:55:15.109
...could I trouble you to carry me
across the loch on your back?

510
00:55:15.311 --> 00:55:16.505
Show me the way home?"

511
00:55:16.713 --> 00:55:20.843
Well, what happens?
Does the water horse help him?

512
00:55:22.886 --> 00:55:23.875
I don't remember.

513
00:55:24.687 --> 00:55:27.588
What? Does the water horse
help him or doesn't he?

514
00:55:27.791 --> 00:55:30.783
Well, it was so long ago
that I heard it.

515
00:55:31.961 --> 00:55:35.556
It either carries him across the loch...

516
00:55:35.765 --> 00:55:38.563
...or it drags him down to the bottom
to his watery death.

517
00:55:40.069 --> 00:55:41.866
I'm not sure which.

518
00:55:56.820 --> 00:55:59.015
Here you go, your favorite snack.

519
00:56:26.316 --> 00:56:27.305
Angus, run now!

520
00:56:29.018 --> 00:56:30.815
Angus, run!

521
00:56:31.821 --> 00:56:34.585
Faster. No looking back.

522
00:56:51.541 --> 00:56:53.236
Mowbray.

523
00:56:54.344 --> 00:56:56.471
You're all wet, man.

524
00:56:56.679 --> 00:56:57.941
You been fishing?

525
00:56:59.782 --> 00:57:04.583
No. I went diving, to see all those
submarines you're going to sink.

526
00:57:04.787 --> 00:57:06.778
Oh, you like to mock the war effort,
do you?

527
00:57:06.990 --> 00:57:09.220
No. No, I don't.

528
00:57:09.425 --> 00:57:12.417
Our forces over there in Europe
on the front lines...

529
00:57:13.296 --> 00:57:15.287
...they're doing a great job.

530
00:57:17.901 --> 00:57:20.631
I want you to stay away from young
Angus. Do you understand?

531
00:57:20.837 --> 00:57:23.032
I consider you a bad influence.

532
00:57:23.239 --> 00:57:25.070
Is that so?

533
00:57:26.309 --> 00:57:27.970
I don't know if you're a spy...

534
00:57:28.177 --> 00:57:31.669
...or a deserter,
or just simply a coward, Mowbray.

535
00:57:31.881 --> 00:57:33.610
- But I'm gonna find out.
- Oh, I see.

536
00:57:33.816 --> 00:57:35.681
They took you away from
the battlefront...

537
00:57:35.885 --> 00:57:39.116
...to hunt for deserters and cowards.
Well, I'm neither.

538
00:57:39.322 --> 00:57:42.052
So you can take that
Sassenach attitude of yours...

539
00:57:42.258 --> 00:57:44.453
...back to where you came from.

540
00:57:45.929 --> 00:57:49.524
You stay away from the boy.
Do you understand?

541
00:57:57.206 --> 00:57:58.901
I'll have a bit of breakfast.

542
00:58:02.645 --> 00:58:04.738
Wait, wait.

543
00:58:05.114 --> 00:58:07.344
- How does she feel?
- Oh, respectable.

544
00:58:07.550 --> 00:58:09.643
- It's a nice one.
- Get the net.

545
00:58:11.287 --> 00:58:13.016
You've got a keeper, for sure.

546
00:58:17.126 --> 00:58:19.856
I'd say it's at least a five-pounder.

547
00:58:26.235 --> 00:58:28.999
- Oh, my sainted mother!
- What on earth is it?

548
00:58:29.205 --> 00:58:31.969
- It's a monster.
- It's a big fish, all right.

549
00:58:32.175 --> 00:58:36.737
No, it's a monster,
a creature with horns. A devil!

550
00:58:36.946 --> 00:58:39.744
I told you to stay off that drink
in the morning.

551
00:58:41.951 --> 00:58:44.146
It's a big fish, all right.

552
00:58:52.462 --> 00:58:56.558
- It's gigantic!
- Oh, no!

553
00:58:58.468 --> 00:59:02.029
Cut the line. Mother of God, cut it.

554
00:59:02.472 --> 00:59:07.466
Are you mad? This is the biggest fish
I've ever had on. I'm going to land it.

555
00:59:19.188 --> 00:59:23.090
- It's going to sink us!
- Okay, okay. Cut the line.

556
00:59:23.292 --> 00:59:24.919
Cut the line before he drowns us.

557
00:59:25.628 --> 00:59:28.927
For God's sake, hurry up. Hurry up!

558
00:59:43.413 --> 00:59:45.244
Did you see it?

559
00:59:46.082 --> 00:59:49.483
I saw it. Whatever it is.

560
00:59:56.192 --> 00:59:57.659
Sir.

561
00:59:58.695 --> 01:00:03.462
Now, young man, your mother has
asked me to enlist you in service.

562
01:00:03.666 --> 01:00:07.329
You are completely aimless, and
we're gonna give you some purpose...

563
01:00:07.537 --> 01:00:09.528
...make a soldier out of you, all right?

564
01:00:14.010 --> 01:00:16.808
Left! Left, right, left!

565
01:00:17.013 --> 01:00:18.537
Left, left!

566
01:00:25.354 --> 01:00:28.949
Come on, lad, hurry up. War'll be over
before you finish that spud.

567
01:00:33.162 --> 01:00:36.996
That's very good, Angus.
Put your back into it, lad.

568
01:00:44.207 --> 01:00:45.970
Left, right, left!

569
01:00:46.175 --> 01:00:50.168
Left, left. Left, right, left!

570
01:00:57.253 --> 01:01:00.518
Keep peeling. I'll be back.

571
01:01:09.866 --> 01:01:14.360
Very good. Now, on to the next one.

572
01:01:17.106 --> 01:01:22.043
I want this vehicle spotless
both inside and out. Right?

573
01:01:22.245 --> 01:01:24.805
- You understand your orders?
- Yes.

574
01:01:25.014 --> 01:01:28.279
You're in the army now. We have
a way of addressing officers, don't we?

575
01:01:28.484 --> 01:01:33.387
- Yes, sir.
- Good man. Well, carry on.

576
01:01:33.589 --> 01:01:35.648
Now, Edwards.

577
01:01:35.858 --> 01:01:38.486
We need to lay down
something for you.

578
01:01:41.364 --> 01:01:45.664
We need to ensure that these grounds
are unaffected by our presence...

579
01:01:48.971 --> 01:01:50.165
You wally.

580
01:01:50.373 --> 01:01:52.466
- Right away, sir.
- Carry on.

581
01:02:23.873 --> 01:02:25.841
Crusoe!

582
01:02:27.109 --> 01:02:28.940
Where are you?

583
01:02:30.012 --> 01:02:32.606
I'm sorry I've not been
to see you for a while.

584
01:02:32.815 --> 01:02:36.114
I haven't been able to get away
until now.

585
01:02:55.004 --> 01:02:57.063
Crusoe!

586
01:02:57.406 --> 01:02:59.397
Crusoe!

587
01:03:01.978 --> 01:03:04.378
Crusoe!

588
01:03:05.815 --> 01:03:08.147
Crusoe!

589
01:03:09.986 --> 01:03:11.977
Where are you?

590
01:03:27.236 --> 01:03:29.227
Crusoe?

591
01:03:34.877 --> 01:03:37.175
Holy mackerel.

592
01:03:45.288 --> 01:03:47.085
Hello, boy.

593
01:03:50.793 --> 01:03:52.420
Careful, don't tip me over.

594
01:03:54.463 --> 01:03:56.761
It's good to see you, boy.

595
01:04:02.505 --> 01:04:04.666
Wait! Don't go!

596
01:04:06.642 --> 01:04:07.631
Crusoe!

597
01:04:16.319 --> 01:04:17.786
Crusoe!

598
01:05:28.858 --> 01:05:31.827
This isn't so bad. It's fun, actually.

599
01:05:38.467 --> 01:05:40.332
Crusoe!

600
01:06:12.868 --> 01:06:14.335
Crusoe!

601
01:07:37.520 --> 01:07:39.920
Crusoe!

602
01:08:18.794 --> 01:08:20.523
What is it, Churchill?

603
01:08:21.130 --> 01:08:22.461
Slow down, boy. Heel!

604
01:08:44.420 --> 01:08:48.379
Shoo, shoo. Go away. Crusoe, go.

605
01:08:54.496 --> 01:08:57.329
Crusoe, go! Shoo!

606
01:09:04.607 --> 01:09:07.701
I thought it was raining, but
when I looked up, there he was.

607
01:09:07.910 --> 01:09:09.104
- No.
- Big as a house.

608
01:09:09.311 --> 01:09:12.974
- So then what happened?
- I held out my hand, "Now, Crusoe...

609
01:09:13.182 --> 01:09:16.618
...you have to promise
to greet me, not eat me. "

610
01:09:16.819 --> 01:09:19.253
- Did he greet you or eat you?
- He ate me.

611
01:09:19.455 --> 01:09:21.116
Yeah, right.
That's why you're here.

612
01:09:21.323 --> 01:09:23.348
Like a sardine.

613
01:09:28.597 --> 01:09:31.088
- There was everything down there.
- Come on.

614
01:09:31.300 --> 01:09:32.528
There was even a shipwreck.

615
01:09:32.735 --> 01:09:34.635
- No.
- Yes.

616
01:09:35.804 --> 01:09:37.294
Yeah?

617
01:09:40.643 --> 01:09:42.975
And I could just see a treasure chest.

618
01:09:43.178 --> 01:09:45.271
And there was money in it.

619
01:09:45.481 --> 01:09:48.609
- It was too far away for me to reach.
- Yeah.

620
01:09:49.618 --> 01:09:51.950
It was amazing.

621
01:09:55.357 --> 01:09:56.847
Ma'am.

622
01:09:57.159 --> 01:09:58.421
Did y- Ma'am?

623
01:10:01.063 --> 01:10:05.124
- Did you need something, ma'am?
- No. No. Thank you.

624
01:10:05.868 --> 01:10:07.130
For what?

625
01:10:08.270 --> 01:10:09.897
For making them laugh.

626
01:10:11.206 --> 01:10:15.074
Haven't heard them laugh like that
in a long time, especially Angus.

627
01:10:15.277 --> 01:10:18.110
- He's a good lad, ma'am.
- Yes, he is.

628
01:10:19.782 --> 01:10:22.250
I used to make him laugh like that.

629
01:10:24.486 --> 01:10:27.080
Maybe you should try again.

630
01:10:29.091 --> 01:10:30.217
What?

631
01:10:32.995 --> 01:10:37.625
Well, just give him
a wee bit more of your time.

632
01:10:41.103 --> 01:10:42.866
Yes.

633
01:10:43.272 --> 01:10:46.173
- Mr. Mowbray.
- Please, call me Lewis.

634
01:10:46.675 --> 01:10:48.506
Where'd you get your scar?

635
01:10:50.312 --> 01:10:52.177
Shrapnel.

636
01:10:54.717 --> 01:10:56.810
Almost took my arm off.

637
01:10:58.921 --> 01:11:02.516
I was a gunner on a torpedo boat.

638
01:11:04.026 --> 01:11:08.656
We were dive-bombed by
a German plane, and I was firing at it.

639
01:11:09.231 --> 01:11:11.722
Next thing, woke up in the hospital.

640
01:11:12.935 --> 01:11:16.302
- Is it still painful?
- It's nothing.

641
01:11:16.505 --> 01:11:20.100
I'm lucky, compared to
what some people have to...

642
01:11:27.249 --> 01:11:29.149
It's a good thing I didn't lose the arm.

643
01:11:31.286 --> 01:11:35.382
I'd not be much of a handyman
with one hand.

644
01:11:35.657 --> 01:11:39.616
You know, I'd be a one-handyman.
Sorry.

645
01:11:42.030 --> 01:11:45.466
No. I'm- I'm sorry. Well-

646
01:11:45.667 --> 01:11:47.965
You will tell me if it's troubling you,
won't you?

647
01:11:54.109 --> 01:11:56.373
- Lieutenant?
- Yes, sir?

648
01:11:56.578 --> 01:11:59.069
We had any news
on this Mowbray character yet?

649
01:11:59.281 --> 01:12:02.808
Yes, sir. I put my report
on your desk yesterday, sir.

650
01:12:03.018 --> 01:12:05.350
Mowbray's as clean as a whistle, sir.

651
01:12:05.554 --> 01:12:08.079
County records show him
born and raised in Dornoch.

652
01:12:08.290 --> 01:12:10.986
Enlisted in the Royal Navy, 1,939.

653
01:12:11.193 --> 01:12:12.956
Unblemished war record.

654
01:12:13.162 --> 01:12:17.326
Injured in action, August 1,940,
and honorably discharged.

655
01:12:17.533 --> 01:12:20.696
He's something of a hero,
you might say, sir.

656
01:12:22.104 --> 01:12:25.005
- Thank you, Wormsley, that'll be all.
- Yes, sir.

657
01:12:33.715 --> 01:12:35.376
The head on it!

658
01:12:35.584 --> 01:12:39.111
And a tail like a crocodile,
only bigger. Wasn't it?

659
01:12:39.321 --> 01:12:42.620
You've been telling that story for
a week, and it changes every time.

660
01:12:42.825 --> 01:12:45.259
- Scales or skin?
- Skin.

661
01:12:45.461 --> 01:12:46.894
What's your interest, Jock?

662
01:12:47.095 --> 01:12:48.323
- Interest?
- Aye.

663
01:12:48.530 --> 01:12:50.395
This is front-page news.

664
01:12:50.599 --> 01:12:53.932
The Aberdeen Argus readers
will want to hear of this.

665
01:12:54.136 --> 01:12:55.899
Not a word of this is to get out.

666
01:12:56.104 --> 01:13:01.167
But Hughie, when the war's over,
there could be tourists, excursions.

667
01:13:01.376 --> 01:13:04.106
This could put our Loch Ness
on the map.

668
01:13:04.313 --> 01:13:07.510
I don't want it on the map.
Just leave things the way they are.

669
01:13:07.716 --> 01:13:11.618
If there's a monster in Loch Ness,
the world has a right to know.

670
01:13:11.820 --> 01:13:13.447
Aye.

671
01:13:20.796 --> 01:13:22.787
Well, it's a perfect day for it.

672
01:13:22.998 --> 01:13:25.592
This is awfully nice of you,
but you don't have to.

673
01:13:25.801 --> 01:13:29.601
No, I want you and the children to see
the important work that we're doing.

674
01:13:36.011 --> 01:13:40.414
If it's a mammal, it can't stay
underwater for six hours.

675
01:13:40.616 --> 01:13:44.575
Mammal, my arse. It's a monster,
it can do what it wants.

676
01:13:44.786 --> 01:13:47.755
You just be ready.
This photograph will make us all rich.

677
01:13:47.956 --> 01:13:49.423
Aye, aye.

678
01:13:51.393 --> 01:13:54.328
Well, the first thing we did
was to cut an access road...

679
01:13:54.530 --> 01:13:56.430
...all the way to the top of the hill.

680
01:13:56.632 --> 01:13:59.760
All the way up to the location. See?

681
01:13:59.968 --> 01:14:00.992
Sir!

682
01:14:01.203 --> 01:14:04.798
I know we've been an inconvenience
for you since we've been here.

683
01:14:05.007 --> 01:14:08.636
But I think you'll be impressed
by what I'm about to show you.

684
01:14:21.890 --> 01:14:26.088
This location, obviously,
has been strategically chosen...

685
01:14:26.295 --> 01:14:28.058
...for the long-range views.

686
01:14:28.263 --> 01:14:32.791
Moray Firth in the east,
Loch Lochy in the west.

687
01:14:33.001 --> 01:14:35.196
- Now follow me.
- Thank you.

688
01:14:35.904 --> 01:14:38.031
Now, here she is...

689
01:14:38.240 --> 01:14:40.834
...our pride and joy.

690
01:14:43.412 --> 01:14:45.277
Now, before we fire up Victoria-

691
01:14:45.480 --> 01:14:47.004
That's what we call her.

692
01:14:47.215 --> 01:14:50.946
- -I need you three
to remain here on the perimeter.

693
01:14:51.153 --> 01:14:55.180
And you might wanna cover your
ears. She packs a hell of a punch.

694
01:14:55.390 --> 01:14:59.884
- Come on. Block your ears.
- We will be firing down the loch.

695
01:15:02.698 --> 01:15:04.893
Let's see what sort of splash
she makes.

696
01:15:10.706 --> 01:15:12.071
All right, men. Walker.

697
01:15:12.341 --> 01:15:14.775
- The loch? No, he can't do that.
- What on earth?

698
01:15:14.977 --> 01:15:17.207
I should have told you,
there's a creature there.

699
01:15:17.412 --> 01:15:19.004
A water horse. He could get hurt.

700
01:15:21.883 --> 01:15:24.408
What? Stop this nonsense
and let them do their work.

701
01:15:24.620 --> 01:15:26.918
- No, Captain Hamilton. You mustn't!
- Angus! Angus!

702
01:15:27.122 --> 01:15:28.419
We're to remain over here.

703
01:15:28.624 --> 01:15:32.116
Mother, please listen. He hatched
from an egg. I saved his life.

704
01:15:32.327 --> 01:15:34.056
- That is enough.
- Mother, it's true.

705
01:15:34.262 --> 01:15:36.287
He's telling the truth.
You have to believe-

706
01:15:36.498 --> 01:15:38.295
- No! Stop!
- Fire!

707
01:15:39.568 --> 01:15:41.058
Fire!

708
01:16:02.024 --> 01:16:03.753
Very good.

709
01:16:04.426 --> 01:16:06.053
What happened?

710
01:16:06.261 --> 01:16:09.287
Did they kill Crusoe?
That can't be the end.

711
01:16:09.498 --> 01:16:11.261
No, no, no.

712
01:16:11.466 --> 01:16:14.162
But it was the beginning of the end.

713
01:16:14.369 --> 01:16:17.805
Poor Angus was beside himself
for his creature.

714
01:16:18.006 --> 01:16:21.066
He got into terrible trouble.

715
01:16:27.249 --> 01:16:29.615
- No! Don't!
- Hey! You!

716
01:16:29.818 --> 01:16:31.410
- No! Don't!
- Control yourself, boy.

717
01:16:31.853 --> 01:16:34.651
Did I or did I not tell you
to wait over there?!

718
01:16:35.791 --> 01:16:40.160
Angus, this is not the place for us.
Can someone drive us home, please?

719
01:16:41.997 --> 01:16:44.329
- Certainly. Corbin.
- Yes, sir.

720
01:16:44.533 --> 01:16:46.865
- Kirstie.
- Anne.

721
01:16:50.172 --> 01:16:52.606
The boy needs discipline, Anne.

722
01:16:54.876 --> 01:16:59.438
The captain lost all respect
in Anne's eyes that day.

723
01:16:59.648 --> 01:17:01.377
All right, Corbin.

724
01:17:07.522 --> 01:17:11.390
That creature'll never surface
after that bombardment. Puddocks.

725
01:17:11.593 --> 01:17:14.858
- You bloody pests!
- Aye.

726
01:17:15.063 --> 01:17:20.160
And he blew a big hole in Jock's plans
for fame and Jimmy's for fortune.

727
01:17:20.368 --> 01:17:22.199
- To the left!
- Jimmy McGarry was a man...

728
01:17:22.404 --> 01:17:25.532
...not to be lightly parted
from a pound or a penny.

729
01:17:25.741 --> 01:17:28.471
- To the right!
- He had seen the monster...

730
01:17:28.677 --> 01:17:31.805
...and he was determined
the rest of the world should see it too.

731
01:17:32.013 --> 01:17:34.174
- Get out of the picture.
- Aye, go!

732
01:17:34.382 --> 01:17:35.542
Off you go.

733
01:17:40.021 --> 01:17:41.454
Aye.

734
01:17:41.656 --> 01:17:45.888
- We have got it.
- We're going to be rich. Rich.

735
01:17:46.094 --> 01:17:49.655
You can come in now,
you daft buggers.

736
01:17:50.198 --> 01:17:52.928
Everyone had a tale to tell
about the monster.

737
01:17:53.602 --> 01:17:58.335
Everyone except poor Angus.

738
01:17:58.540 --> 01:18:01.737
He was sent to bed at 6
every night for a month.

739
01:18:01.943 --> 01:18:03.570
Even his goody-two-shoes sister...

740
01:18:03.779 --> 01:18:06.839
...could not convince their mother
of the truth.

741
01:18:07.048 --> 01:18:10.506
And no one
had laid sight of Crusoe...

742
01:18:10.719 --> 01:18:12.983
...since the bombardment...

743
01:18:13.188 --> 01:18:17.557
...until that fateful night.

744
01:18:20.162 --> 01:18:21.754
Blimey, that's it.

745
01:18:21.963 --> 01:18:24.557
- What's it?
- The monster.

746
01:18:24.766 --> 01:18:26.631
That's just what I saw.

747
01:18:30.071 --> 01:18:33.063
If it's out there, I'll catch it.

748
01:18:34.643 --> 01:18:37.077
Let's go hunting, boys.

749
01:18:42.651 --> 01:18:44.846
- Angus.
- Kirstie?

750
01:18:45.053 --> 01:18:46.452
This is Lewis' idea.

751
01:18:46.655 --> 01:18:50.648
If you tell Mummy I let you out,
I'll deny it. Do you understand?

752
01:18:56.665 --> 01:18:59.065
Now everyone's talking
about seeing the beast.

753
01:18:59.267 --> 01:19:02.236
You have to get him
to hide better or something.

754
01:19:08.577 --> 01:19:10.169
Crusoe!

755
01:19:10.378 --> 01:19:13.939
Crusoe! Come!

756
01:19:21.356 --> 01:19:23.187
Try again.

757
01:19:23.391 --> 01:19:27.020
Crusoe! Come!

758
01:19:49.718 --> 01:19:52.278
Crusoe! Easy, boy, it's me!

759
01:19:54.823 --> 01:19:57.417
- Lewis, what's happened to him?
- He's gone wild, lad.

760
01:19:57.626 --> 01:19:59.651
No. You're wrong.

761
01:20:00.962 --> 01:20:02.361
You'll see.

762
01:20:05.100 --> 01:20:06.192
Angus, be careful.

763
01:20:16.444 --> 01:20:18.742
Easy, boy.

764
01:20:18.947 --> 01:20:21.177
I'm your friend.

765
01:20:27.355 --> 01:20:28.515
No, Angus!

766
01:20:48.376 --> 01:20:52.403
It was your idea to put him in the wild.
You said it would be best for him.

767
01:20:52.614 --> 01:20:55.014
I didn't know that
they'd shoot at him.

768
01:20:55.216 --> 01:20:57.411
This is your fault.

769
01:21:22.811 --> 01:21:24.779
Will it work?

770
01:21:25.213 --> 01:21:28.546
Soft spot. Back of the neck.

771
01:21:28.750 --> 01:21:31.014
Consider it dead.

772
01:21:44.699 --> 01:21:47.065
Did you shoot that plane down?

773
01:21:47.268 --> 01:21:48.565
What?

774
01:21:48.770 --> 01:21:52.035
The plane you were shooting at
when you got hurt.

775
01:21:52.240 --> 01:21:55.038
Well, it's not something I brag about.

776
01:21:56.277 --> 01:21:57.869
Why not?

777
01:22:07.188 --> 01:22:09.452
- Crusoe.
- Angus.

778
01:22:14.129 --> 01:22:15.858
You hear that?

779
01:22:16.364 --> 01:22:18.127
It's Churchill.

780
01:22:18.566 --> 01:22:21.967
I think he's onto something.
Good dog!

781
01:22:22.170 --> 01:22:26.504
It's that dog. He'll tell everyone
where Crusoe is.

782
01:22:33.381 --> 01:22:37.875
- Wormsley, over there. In that cove.
- I'll take her in.

783
01:23:08.216 --> 01:23:09.376
Kill the engine.

784
01:23:11.653 --> 01:23:13.314
Where's he gone?

785
01:23:14.189 --> 01:23:16.089
Churchill?

786
01:23:16.357 --> 01:23:18.291
Oi, Churchill!

787
01:23:21.296 --> 01:23:23.856
Maybe he's just
chasing a rabbit or something.

788
01:23:24.732 --> 01:23:26.029
That's not it.

789
01:23:39.013 --> 01:23:41.447
Someone kill it! Kill it!

790
01:23:46.654 --> 01:23:51.182
Mayday! Mayday! We're on the loch.
And we're under attack!

791
01:23:56.464 --> 01:23:59.865
- Where is he?
- There it is, port side.

792
01:24:03.671 --> 01:24:06.435
Shoot it! Shoot it!

793
01:24:08.243 --> 01:24:10.507
Turn the boat around.

794
01:24:11.279 --> 01:24:13.270
Where is it? Where is it?

795
01:24:48.917 --> 01:24:50.612
Anne.

796
01:24:51.686 --> 01:24:52.880
Look, I...

797
01:24:53.821 --> 01:24:57.279
I don't wish to carry on like this.
I apologize for my actions on the hill.

798
01:24:57.492 --> 01:25:00.017
No, no. We should apologize.

799
01:25:00.228 --> 01:25:03.857
Angus should never have behaved
in such an inappropriate fashion.

800
01:25:04.065 --> 01:25:05.794
- Sir!
- I do-

801
01:25:06.301 --> 01:25:09.361
Sir! We just had a call from the loch.
We're under attack.

802
01:25:11.039 --> 01:25:12.870
- What?
- We're under attack, sir.

803
01:25:13.074 --> 01:25:14.974
- Get me a sub. This could be it!
- Right.

804
01:25:15.176 --> 01:25:17.235
- Bring my jeep around to the front.
- Yes, sir.

805
01:25:17.445 --> 01:25:19.606
You take the children into the cellar.
Wormsley?

806
01:25:19.814 --> 01:25:22.112
Kirstie, Angus, downstairs now!

807
01:25:23.318 --> 01:25:25.445
- Angus is- He's not here.
- What?

808
01:25:25.653 --> 01:25:28.645
- He's gone down to the loch.
- Oh, my God.

809
01:25:28.856 --> 01:25:31.290
Come quickly. Hurry, downstairs.
Go, go, go.

810
01:25:31.492 --> 01:25:32.959
- This is not a drill.
- Yes, sir.

811
01:25:33.161 --> 01:25:36.653
Clyde, bring the car round to the front
of the house straightaway.

812
01:25:36.864 --> 01:25:40.766
- I can't let you. It's too dangerous.
- He's my son. You try to stop me.

813
01:25:49.544 --> 01:25:51.444
Go away from me!

814
01:25:52.313 --> 01:25:54.873
Go away. Go away from me!

815
01:25:58.186 --> 01:26:01.246
Oh, my God. Please.

816
01:26:03.358 --> 01:26:05.588
No. No, please!

817
01:26:08.062 --> 01:26:12.192
- Crusoe!
- Go the other way. Turn around.

818
01:26:12.400 --> 01:26:14.095
Please! Go.

819
01:26:21.876 --> 01:26:24.811
Crusoe! Stop! Put him down!

820
01:26:25.213 --> 01:26:27.841
- Oh, go away!
- Stop it!

821
01:26:30.952 --> 01:26:33.853
- Crusoe! Stop!
- Angus, no!

822
01:26:41.496 --> 01:26:42.793
Crusoe!

823
01:26:45.533 --> 01:26:46.932
No!

824
01:26:52.206 --> 01:26:55.573
- Somebody! Get off me! Help.
- Crusoe?

825
01:27:37.719 --> 01:27:41.416
Angus? Can you hear me?

826
01:27:41.656 --> 01:27:45.888
Come on, lad. Come on, son.
Come on, Angus, breathe.

827
01:27:46.427 --> 01:27:47.894
Come on!

828
01:27:48.529 --> 01:27:53.159
If you have any magic in you at all,
use it on this lad, I'm begging you.

829
01:27:58.373 --> 01:28:00.273
Put the gunners on high alert,
understand?

830
01:28:00.475 --> 01:28:03.035
Any sign of an enemy vessel,
you fire at will.

831
01:28:03.244 --> 01:28:06.441
- But, sir-
- No. You fire at will!

832
01:28:10.852 --> 01:28:14.253
Come on. Come on, lad.

833
01:28:14.455 --> 01:28:15.888
Come on. Breathe, Angus.

834
01:28:16.090 --> 01:28:18.615
Come on, get some good Scottish air
in those lungs.

835
01:28:19.694 --> 01:28:21.753
Stay with me, Angus.
Stay with me, lad.

836
01:28:21.963 --> 01:28:26.195
Come on. Come on, lad, breathe.
Come on.

837
01:28:35.343 --> 01:28:38.403
Come on, lad.
Come on, we should go now.

838
01:28:40.948 --> 01:28:45.180
Know something? This old workshop
is the place I'll miss the most.

839
01:28:47.221 --> 01:28:48.654
You'll look after it for me?

840
01:28:54.762 --> 01:28:57.731
And I'll bet you grow into these
by the time I get back.

841
01:29:02.870 --> 01:29:04.861
You keep them well-polished.

842
01:29:13.915 --> 01:29:16.076
We'll have none of that.

843
01:29:16.417 --> 01:29:20.683
You're the man of the house now.
I need you to be strong and sure.

844
01:29:21.222 --> 01:29:24.623
All right, Dad. Strong and sure.

845
01:29:25.126 --> 01:29:27.390
Aye. That's my boy.

846
01:29:33.167 --> 01:29:36.728
That's it. That's it.
Good lad. That's it.

847
01:29:37.438 --> 01:29:39.167
Good boy.

848
01:29:42.176 --> 01:29:43.837
Crusoe?

849
01:29:51.819 --> 01:29:54.287
That's a good water horse.

850
01:30:01.262 --> 01:30:03.389
- Thanks. Oh, God.
- Captain Hamilton!

851
01:30:04.031 --> 01:30:04.998
Angus!

852
01:30:05.199 --> 01:30:07.224
- He's all right.
- Angus, what happened?

853
01:30:07.435 --> 01:30:09.232
- You got to kill it.
- What's happening?

854
01:30:09.437 --> 01:30:12.372
- I was only trying to save Crusoe.
- Who's Crusoe?

855
01:30:12.607 --> 01:30:15.269
- Wouldn't believe me if I told you.
- It's a sea monster.

856
01:30:15.476 --> 01:30:16.443
It was just there-

857
01:30:16.644 --> 01:30:19.807
- What are you talking about?
- I swear it. He was just there.

858
01:30:21.616 --> 01:30:25.916
A sea monster? Are you telling me
this has all been about a sea monster?

859
01:30:27.755 --> 01:30:29.052
What is going on here?

860
01:30:29.991 --> 01:30:33.449
Has everyone gone mad?!
A sea monster?

861
01:30:35.530 --> 01:30:37.293
There is no monster.

862
01:30:37.498 --> 01:30:39.728
There's just you
with your bloody gun!

863
01:30:41.002 --> 01:30:42.526
And you.

864
01:30:42.737 --> 01:30:47.197
Filling his head with tales
of sea creatures and magic?

865
01:30:47.775 --> 01:30:51.677
There's no monster.
And there's no magic.

866
01:30:52.146 --> 01:30:54.944
- Mother...
- There's just-

867
01:30:56.918 --> 01:31:01.355
There's just this war, and death...

868
01:31:02.023 --> 01:31:04.491
...and people acting insane.

869
01:31:05.893 --> 01:31:08.293
What's wrong with everyone?

870
01:31:35.890 --> 01:31:37.152
Angus?

871
01:31:37.358 --> 01:31:40.691
- Yes, Mum. I tried to tell you.
- Yes, you did.

872
01:31:40.895 --> 01:31:44.558
His name's Crusoe. I raised him,
right out of an egg.

873
01:31:44.765 --> 01:31:46.096
Angus, be careful!

874
01:31:46.300 --> 01:31:49.929
It's all right. He's my friend.
He's peaceful, really.

875
01:31:50.137 --> 01:31:53.834
He's not peaceful at all, sir.
The damn thing tried to eat me.

876
01:32:04.251 --> 01:32:06.185
Crusoe! Get out of here!

877
01:32:07.588 --> 01:32:08.885
Crusoe, come!

878
01:32:10.491 --> 01:32:11.480
Crusoe!

879
01:32:12.994 --> 01:32:16.987
- Oh, my God.
- Crusoe, come!

880
01:32:17.398 --> 01:32:18.797
Good boy.

881
01:32:19.000 --> 01:32:21.662
- Come on. Over here.
- No!

882
01:32:23.371 --> 01:32:24.360
Angus!

883
01:32:25.706 --> 01:32:28.266
Angus! Angus!

884
01:32:42.923 --> 01:32:46.120
Faster, Crusoe!
We have to get to the net!

885
01:32:48.729 --> 01:32:50.890
The sea. He's trying to
get Crusoe out to sea.

886
01:32:51.098 --> 01:32:53.225
Victoria base, come in.

887
01:32:54.035 --> 01:32:56.902
Victoria base, come in.

888
01:32:57.271 --> 01:32:59.603
You have to do something.
You have to stop them.

889
01:32:59.807 --> 01:33:02.970
All the radios are down.
We have to get to the net.

890
01:33:09.016 --> 01:33:11.883
All ammunition up here.
On the double!

891
01:33:14.021 --> 01:33:15.921
Help!

892
01:33:17.792 --> 01:33:19.623
No! No.

893
01:33:26.133 --> 01:33:28.158
You were in the Royal Navy.
That's right?

894
01:33:28.369 --> 01:33:30.803
- Aye, sir.
- Think you can handle this tub?

895
01:33:44.051 --> 01:33:48.681
I see the channel. Now head to sea,
boy, as fast as you can.

896
01:34:06.307 --> 01:34:09.037
- Come on, keep it moving.
- I see something.

897
01:34:09.710 --> 01:34:14.511
The rain's too heavy to make it out
clearly. It looks like a periscope.

898
01:34:15.516 --> 01:34:20.385
Good God, it is. A German sub.
Prepare to fire.

899
01:34:27.795 --> 01:34:29.558
Fire!

900
01:34:33.134 --> 01:34:36.900
Go under, Crusoe.
We have to go under!

901
01:34:59.426 --> 01:35:00.450
- There!
- Where?

902
01:35:00.661 --> 01:35:03.653
- Over there! Can you see them?
- I can't see him.

903
01:35:10.070 --> 01:35:11.367
- Rapp! Situation report?
- Sir.

904
01:35:11.572 --> 01:35:14.564
One of our own torpedo boats
is in pursuit of the target.

905
01:35:14.775 --> 01:35:18.802
They're on the run. Don't let them
get to the sea. Raise the nets!

906
01:35:35.129 --> 01:35:37.597
Faster, Crusoe!

907
01:35:42.236 --> 01:35:44.397
Go, Crusoe! Go!

908
01:36:23.210 --> 01:36:24.973
What are we going to do?

909
01:36:26.213 --> 01:36:28.841
- Angus!
- Over here!

910
01:36:29.049 --> 01:36:30.414
Angus!

911
01:36:30.618 --> 01:36:34.349
- Let him go!
- Do as he says!

912
01:36:34.555 --> 01:36:36.489
Go on, boy!

913
01:36:37.791 --> 01:36:40.259
Angus, let him go!

914
01:36:40.861 --> 01:36:42.920
Let go, Angus!

915
01:36:43.497 --> 01:36:45.488
Let go!

916
01:36:48.168 --> 01:36:51.069
You're the best friend I've ever had.

917
01:37:03.450 --> 01:37:05.975
I'll never forget you.

918
01:37:30.010 --> 01:37:35.277
No, Crusoe! You can't stay here!
Go and hide in the loch.

919
01:37:36.450 --> 01:37:38.441
Crusoe, go!

920
01:37:40.120 --> 01:37:43.453
Don't you hear me, you bloody fool?
Do as I say.

921
01:37:43.724 --> 01:37:44.713
Go!

922
01:37:55.669 --> 01:37:57.534
Come on!

923
01:37:59.440 --> 01:38:01.533
- Come on, Angus.
- You're nearly there.

924
01:38:02.376 --> 01:38:03.741
- Come on.
- Come on, lad.

925
01:38:05.646 --> 01:38:08.274
Now give me your hand.
Give me your hand, boy. Come on.

926
01:38:08.482 --> 01:38:10.450
Get him in the boat.

927
01:38:11.218 --> 01:38:13.482
That's it. Good lad.

928
01:38:15.356 --> 01:38:18.189
Where is he? Where'd he go?

929
01:38:25.733 --> 01:38:26.791
There he is!

930
01:38:42.282 --> 01:38:44.409
- He's gonna jump the net!
- It's too high!

931
01:38:44.618 --> 01:38:46.518
He'll never make it.

932
01:39:22.556 --> 01:39:26.856
You did it, Crusoe! Well done, boy!

933
01:40:09.436 --> 01:40:12.735
He'll not be coming back, will he?

934
01:40:14.608 --> 01:40:17.702
I think he's meant to be
on his own, dear.

935
01:40:18.679 --> 01:40:21.876
Not Crusoe. I meant...

936
01:40:22.883 --> 01:40:24.578
...Daddy.

937
01:40:30.424 --> 01:40:33.416
No, love. He'll not.

938
01:40:42.202 --> 01:40:44.762
- Look! Look, Angus! Up here.
- Up here!

939
01:40:44.972 --> 01:40:46.633
- You have to come quickly.
- Look!

940
01:40:46.840 --> 01:40:48.102
Come on, quickly.

941
01:41:33.954 --> 01:41:36.889
Some say the creature
comes back from time to time...

942
01:41:37.090 --> 01:41:39.183
...looking for his friend.

943
01:41:39.459 --> 01:41:42.758
Some claim to have seen it
over the years.

944
01:41:42.963 --> 01:41:47.696
But the boy never saw it again.

945
01:41:50.204 --> 01:41:52.138
That's a wonderful story.

946
01:41:52.339 --> 01:41:56.332
Aye. And all true, every word of it.

947
01:41:56.843 --> 01:41:58.504
Thank you...

948
01:41:59.246 --> 01:42:01.146
Angus.

949
01:42:01.515 --> 01:42:04.006
Angus MacMorrow is the name.

950
01:42:05.652 --> 01:42:07.916
He was so sweet. I know it's true.

951
01:42:08.121 --> 01:42:10.851
- It's a great story, but it's not true.
- You are so cynical.

952
01:42:11.058 --> 01:42:12.616
William?

953
01:42:13.560 --> 01:42:15.425
William!

954
01:42:21.635 --> 01:42:23.330
William!

955
01:42:25.472 --> 01:42:26.734
William...

956
01:42:26.940 --> 01:42:29.204
...where are you?
